			},
			"date": "2023-08-31T20:39:39.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@sarajw\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>sarajw</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://yatil.social/@yatil\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>yatil</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@hmig\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>hmig</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span>  I added a theme slider instead of a switch to my site, as a joke initially. But in the light of finding a brightness/contrast sweet spot, this pattern could be useful?</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "110986175378316738",
			"parentId": "110986158360036355",
